Sections "COLLECTION" contain missmatched items: {'COLLECTION_SUMMARY': ["Two almond fields were established in southern Spain in October 2020 with the cultivar 'Avijor' ('Lauranne') grafted onto the almond × peach hybrid rootstock G×N. Almond plants in Cortijo de la Reina (hereafter La Reina; 37°49′15.2″N, 4°53′52.4″W; 98.5 metres above sea level (m a.s.l.)) were planted at 7 × 4\u202fm spacing, and those in El Sotillo (hereafter Sotillo; 37°48′35.9″N, 4°57′01.5″W; 105.1\u202fm a.s.l.) at 6.5 × 4\u202fm spacing. Trees at both sites were established on 30–40\u202fcm high ridges. Drip irrigation was applied via two lateral lines per row (0.75\u202fm spacing, 1.6\u202fL\u202fh⁻¹ per emitter), with annual volumes ranging from 3,000 (2023) to 5,000\u202fm³\u202fha⁻¹ (2024 and 2025) depending on water availability and drought conditions, and fertilization was done through fertigation from March to July (N: 100 kg ha⁻¹; P :15 kg ha⁻¹; K: 90 kg ha⁻¹; Mg: 10 kg ha⁻¹; S: 10 kg ha⁻¹; B: 2 kg ha⁻¹; Zn: 3 kg ha⁻¹; Fe: 5 kg ha⁻¹; Mn: 2 kg ha⁻¹). Soils at both sites derive from Quaternary and Cenozoic continental deposits, comprising conglomerates, sandstones, gravels, sands, silts, and clays. Coarse elements are less abundant in La Reina, where vertic features such as pronounced dry-season cracking indicate the presence of expansive clays (smectites). This soil is classified as a vertic Cambisol (IUSS Working Group WRB, 2015). Sotillo is located on a coarse-fragmented terrace soil, in a Luvisol (IUSS Working Group WRB, 2015). Both experimental sites were located less than 7 km from the IFAPA Alameda del Obispo agrometeorological station (https://www.juntadeandalucia.es/agriculturaypesca/ifapa/riaweb/web/estacion/14/6). Both experimental fields are under the influence of Mediterranean climate, which is characterized by hot, dry summers, high interannual precipitation variability, and annual rainfall below evaporative demand. Long-term (2005–2024) mean annual temperature was 17.8\u202f°C, with mean January minimum of 3.2\u202f°C and July maximum of 36.9\u202f°C. Average annual precipitation in this period was 606\u202fmm, while mean annual reference evapotranspiration (ET₀, FAO Penman-Monteith; Allen et al., 1998) was 1,473\u202fmm\u202fyr⁻¹. A field experiment was established using a randomized complete block design with three blocks and nine treatments (T1–T9), resulting in 27 experimental units per field. Each block included the application of eight commercial microbial biostimulants (T2–T9) and one untreated control (T1), with each treatment applied to five trees constituting the experimental unit. The microbial biostimulants differed in their microbial composition, application rate, and primary mode of action, including nitrogen fixation, phosphorus solubilization, nutrient solubilization, and phytohormone production. The microbial biostimulants were applied by injection into the wetted root zone at a depth of approximately 15 cm during the first week of April 2023, 2024, and 2025 in both fields. For data interpretation throughout the manuscript, treatments were grouped according to their predominant mode of action, as reported by the manufacturers, into nitrogen-fixing (T2, T5, T6, and T9), combined nitrogen-fixing and phosphorus-solubilizing (T3 and T7), phosphorus-solubilizing (T4), nutrient-solubilizing (T8), and the untreated control (T1). To minimize border effects, a one-tree-row buffer was maintained between adjacent treatments within each block and between blocks.", "Two almond fields were established in southern Spain in October 2020 with the cultivar ''Avijor'' (''Lauranne'') grafted onto the almond × peach hybrid rootstock G×N. Almond plants in Cortijo de la Reina (hereafter La Reina; 37°49′15.2″N, 4°53′52.4″W; 98.5 metres above sea level (m a.s.l.)) were planted at 7 × 4\u202fm spacing, and those in El Sotillo (hereafter Sotillo; 37°48′35.9″N, 4°57′01.5″W; 105.1\u202fm a.s.l.) at 6.5 × 4\u202fm spacing. Trees at both sites 